WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.798
When Rosa applies for disability coverage, the insurer collects personal information

00:00:04.798 --> 00:00:09.159
from her, her physicians, consumer reporting agencies and the MIB.

00:00:09.159 --> 00:00:13.957
Maryland’s insurance information and privacy protection law requires the insurer to

00:00:13.957 --> 00:00:18.755
tell her, in a notice of information practices, what kinds of

00:00:18.755 --> 00:00:23.552
information may be collected, from whom, how it may be used

00:00:23.552 --> 00:00:27.914
and disclosed, and her rights to see and correct it.

00:00:27.914 --> 00:00:33.584
Investigative consumer reports require their own notice and the right to be interviewed.

00:00:33.584 --> 00:00:37.073
Pretext interviews, in which someone pretends to be

00:00:37.073 --> 00:00:40.562
another person to pry information loose, are prohibited.

00:00:40.562 --> 00:00:45.360
The law applies to insurers and to producers acting for them.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.082
An adverse underwriting decision is a declination, a termination, a rating or restriction, or

00:00:50.082 --> 00:00:54.466
placement with a different insurer or at a higher rate than applied for.

00:00:54.466 --> 00:00:58.851
When Rosa’s disability policy comes back rated with an exclusion, the insurer must

00:00:58.851 --> 00:01:03.235
give her written notice of the adverse decision that either states the specific

00:01:03.235 --> 00:01:07.620
reasons or tells her she may request them in writing, along with notice

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.993
of her right to access and correct the information used.

00:01:10.993 --> 00:01:15.040
On request the insurer must disclose the specific reasons and the items

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.087
of information that supported them, within the time the law sets, releasing

00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:22.460
medical information through a physician she names if it prefers.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:27.455
Rosa may ask to see the recorded personal information the insurer holds about her,

00:01:27.455 --> 00:01:32.449
and the insurer must provide it or a summary, identify the sources, and name

00:01:32.449 --> 00:01:36.730
the people it has disclosed the information to in the recent past.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.655
If something is wrong, she may request correction, amendment or deletion.

00:01:40.655 --> 00:01:44.579
The insurer either corrects the record and notifies anyone who received

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:48.503
the wrong information, or explains its refusal in writing and lets

00:01:48.503 --> 00:01:52.428
her file a statement of dispute that travels with the record.

00:01:52.428 --> 00:01:56.709
These rights matter most when an MIB code or an inspection report

00:01:56.709 --> 00:02:00.990
contains an error that would otherwise follow her to every future application.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:06.939
Maryland’s privacy regulations, modeled on the federal Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, govern nonpublic personal financial information.

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:12.462
A licensee gives a clear initial privacy notice when a customer relationship begins

00:02:12.462 --> 00:02:17.986
and an annual notice while it continues, with an exception when the licensee

00:02:17.986 --> 00:02:23.085
shares only within the permitted categories and its practices have not changed.

00:02:23.085 --> 00:02:28.184
Before sharing information with a nonaffiliated third party for marketing, the licensee

00:02:28.184 --> 00:02:33.283
must give an opt-out notice and a reasonable opportunity to respond, such

00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:38.381
as 30 days for a mailed notice, and must honor the opt-out.

00:02:38.381 --> 00:02:44.330
Sharing needed to service the transaction, pay claims or comply with law fits exceptions.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.738
Nonpublic personal health information is treated more strictly.

00:02:47.738 --> 00:02:53.701
A licensee may not disclose it without the individual’s written authorization, which must describe

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:59.664
the information, the recipients, the purpose and its expiration, unless an insurance-function exception applies:

00:02:59.664 --> 00:03:04.776
underwriting, claims administration, utilization review, fraud investigation, legal compliance and similar functions.

00:03:04.776 --> 00:03:09.036
Those exceptions let the insurer send Rosa’s records to its

00:03:09.036 --> 00:03:13.295
underwriter; they do not let Theo mention her diagnosis to

00:03:13.295 --> 00:03:17.129
her employer or post it in a sales chat.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:20.962
Federal HIPAA privacy rules add parallel duties for health

00:03:20.962 --> 00:03:24.370
plans and the people working on their behalf.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.337
Follow Rosa’s file.

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:29.852
At application Theo gives her the notice of information practices and the privacy notice.

00:03:29.852 --> 00:03:32.754
The insurer collects her records under her authorization and

00:03:32.754 --> 00:03:35.334
orders an inspection report with the required notice.

00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:38.559
The policy comes back rated; the insurer sends an adverse

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:41.784
underwriting notice with the reasons or how to request them.

00:03:41.784 --> 00:03:45.008
Rosa requests her file, finds an MIB code from a

00:03:45.008 --> 00:03:47.911
condition that was ruled out, and gets it corrected.

00:03:47.911 --> 00:03:51.135
Her medical details never leave the insurance functions, and she

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:54.360
opts out of marketing sharing when the annual notice arrives.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.940
Every step is a rule in this lesson.
